WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
All day, posture and activity push your arterial pressure around.

2
00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.300
Within seconds, a reflex pushes back, but it never quite finishes.

3
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.400
So what decides where pressure settles?

4
00:00:15.983 --> 00:00:21.463
An adult's mean arterial pressure at rest is about 93 mmHg.

5
00:00:22.903 --> 00:00:29.643
Call it 100. Squeeze the small arteries, and with no reflex, it would climb to 120.

6
00:00:31.183 --> 00:00:33.523
That rise of 20 is the disturbance.

7
00:00:34.783 --> 00:00:39.443
But stretch receptors sit in the carotid sinuses, in the neck, and in the aortic

8
00:00:39.443 --> 00:00:43.003
arch. They fire faster as the walls stretch.

9
00:00:44.117 --> 00:00:49.277
Their signals reach the brainstem, which turns vagal output to the heart up, and

10
00:00:49.277 --> 00:00:50.817
sympathetic output down.

11
00:00:52.217 --> 00:00:56.997
The heart slows. Small arteries relax, so resistance falls.

12
00:00:58.517 --> 00:01:00.517
Veins relax, so less blood returns.

13
00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:04.957
Pressure settles near 106.7.

14
00:01:06.157 --> 00:01:10.217
The reflex removed 13.3 and left 6.7.

15
00:01:11.597 --> 00:01:14.377
Correction divided by what is left gives a gain of 2.

16
00:01:15.417 --> 00:01:18.677
A gain of 2 removes two thirds of a fast disturbance.

17
00:01:19.937 --> 00:01:23.197
The further pressure strays, the harder the reflex pushes.

18
00:01:24.577 --> 00:01:26.217
So why does it stop short of 100?

19
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.480
Its push is proportional to the error it still sees.

20
00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.820
At 100, the error would be zero, and so would the push.

21
00:01:40.883 --> 00:01:42.383
Here is the one idea.

22
00:01:43.683 --> 00:01:48.163
In Guyton's framework, the textbook account, two controllers answer two questions.

23
00:01:49.443 --> 00:01:51.883
The reflex decides how far pressure strays.

24
00:01:53.183 --> 00:01:55.543
The kidney decides where pressure ends up.

25
00:01:56.483 --> 00:01:58.683
Switch clocks, from seconds to days.

26
00:01:59.943 --> 00:02:01.123
The squeeze stays on.

27
00:02:02.363 --> 00:02:06.623
As pressure rises, the kidney excretes more sodium, and water follows.

28
00:02:08.023 --> 00:02:10.363
Plotted, the kidney's line climbs with pressure.

29
00:02:11.433 --> 00:02:15.133
The salt you eat is a flat line, the same at every pressure.

30
00:02:16.393 --> 00:02:17.813
The two lines cross once.

31
00:02:19.113 --> 00:02:22.113
Only at that crossing does salt out equal salt in.

32
00:02:23.133 --> 00:02:26.553
Above it, the body loses salt and water every day.

33
00:02:27.933 --> 00:02:30.753
Volume shrinks, less blood returns, and pressure falls.

34
00:02:32.233 --> 00:02:35.913
Lower pressure means less excretion, so each day's loss shrinks.

35
00:02:36.917 --> 00:02:39.017
But the loss stops only at the crossing.

36
00:02:40.237 --> 00:02:43.017
The squeeze is still on, so the heart now pumps less.

37
00:02:44.237 --> 00:02:47.037
The reflex left an error; the kidney leaves none.

38
00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:52.020
The kidney keeps changing volume while any imbalance remains.

39
00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:54.940
Guyton called that an infinite gain.

40
00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:59.120
It is slow, but in his framework, it has the final say.

41
00:03:00.183 --> 00:03:04.423
So the nerves answer within seconds, and the kidney answers over days.

42
00:03:05.823 --> 00:03:09.583
Whether the reflex also helps set the level over weeks is still argued.
